  "worked_solution": "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/b9dbed9bab9d769f7408006854bd0c159cb980bfa5f4441fe1b4e5753dfbc078.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(11,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(15,7).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-8}{15-11} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =-0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(11,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+b \\\\ 8 & =-0.25(11)+b \\\\ 8 & =-2.75+b \\\\ b & =10.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+10.75.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=23$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+10.75 \\\\  & =-0.25(23)+10.75 \\\\  & =-5.75+10.75 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=23$ we have $y≈5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=23$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
